  "about": "Shared byte for byte by the R and Python suites of scopusflow. Each case is one name as it stands in a record's authors column, with the form both twins write to an RIS AU line and to a BibTeX author field. Scopus's dc:creator gives 'Surname I.' with no comma, which BibTeX and biber read as given name first and RIS readers keep as a single field, so a name whose final run is initials gains a comma before them. An initial is an upper-case letter, optionally followed by one lower-case letter (Yu., Ch.), then a full stop. Names with a comma, and names given first, are left as they are. BibTeX also takes run-together initials (J.R.) for one given name, so the BibTeX form spaces them (J. R.) while keeping a hyphen (J.-P.). Most inputs come from the verifier's 21 test vectors. 'John A.' is inverted on purpose, the cost of a rule that cannot tell it from a Scopus surname, which Scopus does not send. The Cyrillic pair holds the rule to Unicode case, not ASCII.",
